# Git decides "binary" by sniffing the first 8k of a blob for a NUL byte. One
# stray control character in a source file is therefore enough to make `git
# diff` refuse to render the change at all — and everything built on top of it
# (`--numstat`, review tools, the harness's own Changes tab) reports the file
# as unreviewable rather than as the text it plainly is.
#
# That is not hypothetical here: the committed `apps/cli/src/bin.ts` carries a
# literal 0x00 inside a string literal at offset 7370, where `"\0"` was meant.
# The byte is in published history, so it cannot be taken back out without
# rewriting a commit that is already on the remote. Setting the `diff`
# attribute fixes the symptom for good instead: git treats these paths as text
# "even when they contain byte values that normally never appear in text
# files, such as NUL" (gitattributes(5)).
#
# Only `diff` is set, deliberately. Adding `text` would enable end-of-line
# normalization for every matching path, which rewrites working copies on the
# next checkout — a much larger change than this one is trying to make.

*.ts    diff
*.tsx   diff
*.cts   diff
*.mts   diff
*.js    diff
*.mjs   diff
*.cjs   diff
*.json  diff
*.jsonc diff
*.css   diff
*.html  diff
*.md    diff
*.yml   diff
*.yaml  diff
